// @test: typecheck-fail
// @level: L0
// @tier: 0
// @tags: modules, name-resolution, module-path, call
// @expected-error: E100
//
// Pin (T0806) — a call through a module path must name something.
//
// The two-segment form of exactly this mistake IS diagnosed:
//
//     m.absent()          ->  error<E100>: unbound variable: absent
//
// so the gap is not "module calls are unchecked" but a length threshold.
// At three segments the same mistake is accepted silently, and the call
// evaluates to `nil` — which satisfies whatever return type the caller
// declared. A typo becomes a wrong VALUE rather than a compile error.
//
// Measured 2026-08-18, each line with a positive control in the same batch:
//
//     m.absent()             two segments, real module        E100
//     zz.absent()            two segments, unknown root       E100
//     "x".absent_method()    missing method on a value        E400
//     p.absent_field         missing field on a record        E404
//     p.sub.deep.absent()    three segments FROM A VALUE      E404
//     a.b.f() wrong type     real path, wrong declared type   E400
//
//     a.b.absent()           three segments, missing LEAF     silent -> nil
//     a.zzz.f()              three segments, missing MIDDLE   silent -> nil
//     zz.a.b.c.d.absent()    six segments                     silent -> nil
//
// Everything adjacent is diagnosed well, which is why this survived: read
// the code and name resolution looks like it works.
//
// The modules here are declared in this file so the spec depends on no
// archive state. `deeper.reachable` is called first and must resolve —
// were the fix to reject good paths along with bad ones, this file would
// still fail, but for the wrong reason, so the companion spec
// `module_path_call_control.vr` pins the good path passing on its own.

module outer {
    public module deeper {
        public fn reachable() -> Int { 7 }
    }
}

fn main() {
    let good: Int = outer.deeper.reachable();
    let bad: Int = outer.deeper.not_declared_anywhere();
    print(f"{good} {bad}");
}
